P(f"On the test set (Table 5, Figure 3a), persistence had an MAE of {f0(h.loc[pe,'MAE'])} t CO₂ per facility-month (WAPE {f1(h.loc[pe,'WAPE'])}%). The seasonal-naive forecast ({rel(sn):+.1f}%) and the moving average ({rel('Moving average (3)'):+.1f}%) were slightly worse than persistence. Every learner improved on persistence: random forest by {abs(rel(rf)):.1f}% (MAE {f0(h.loc[rf,'MAE'])} t; WAPE {f1(h.loc[rf,'WAPE'])}%), gradient boosting by {abs(rel(hg)):.1f}%, linear regression by {abs(rel(lr)):.1f}%, and the change variants by {abs(rel(rc)):.1f}% and {abs(rel(hc)):.1f}%. Linear regression and the gradient-boosting change model had the lowest RMSE (about {f0(h.loc[hc,'RMSE'])} t versus {f0(h.loc[pe,'RMSE'])} t for persistence), which shows that the choice of the “best” model depends on whether typical or large errors matter. R² was high for all methods (persistence {f3(h.loc[pe,'R2'])}; learners {f3(min(h.loc[m,'R2'] for m in [lr,rf,hg,rc,hc]))}–{f3(max(h.loc[m,'R2'] for m in [lr,rf,hg,rc,hc]))}) and is dominated by plant size, so we rely on MAE and its ratio to persistence. The mean of MASE was unstable because a few facilities have very small naive errors, so the median is reported. The seasonal-ratio naive forecast failed (RMSE {f0(h.loc['Seasonal-ratio naive','RMSE'])} t) because ratios of small quantities are unstable.")
