# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5 Discussion
H1("5. Discussion")
P(f"**What the models achieve.** Under a time-ordered design with feature and model choice confined to a validation window, learners that combine current emissions, the seasonal lag and calendar information reduce one-month-ahead absolute error by roughly {ml_lo:.0f}–{max(abs(rel(m)) for m in [lr,rf,hg,rc,hc]):.0f}% relative to persistence. The gain is concentrated in months with a predictable change in output and in larger plants, while the extended benchmark shows that random forest mainly reduces rare large errors and can be worse for small plants or zero-emission months. It is modest rather than universal, and a seasonal-naive forecast is better than the learners for some national-total comparisons. Practically, a facility-level learner is useful for plant-by-plant ranking and anomaly screening, but for the national total a seasonal-naive forecast is hard to beat.")
P("**What the series is.** The audit and the external comparison qualify what is being forecast. A non-trivial share of facility-months repeat the previous year's value, and the national total moves in step with official production within the year but not between years. This is what one would expect if a stable seasonal profile is applied to slowly changing annual levels, as the documented construction methods allow [2]. We cannot tell from the data whether the inventory understates real growth, whether the studied plants differ from national production, or whether emission intensity fell; each is plausible. The implication is clear, however: skill in forecasting this series should not be read as skill in forecasting real emissions. It is skill in anticipating the inventory.")
P("**Where the forecasts are useful.** Within that limit, three uses are reasonable. First, anomaly screening: a facility-month falling outside a calibrated interval is a candidate for review of data quality or of the underlying activity estimate. Second, gap filling and nowcasting: months that are not yet released can be provisionally estimated with stated uncertainty. Third, benchmarking: the baselines and the validation protocol provide a reference for later work using process or production data. The forecasts are not suited to assessing compliance or progress under the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, which require measured or verified facility data.")
P("**Relation to earlier work.** Studies that forecast cement emissions with machine learning have reported strong gains from learners, but have used either plant process data [3] or industry totals [4]. Our results agree that tree ensembles are competitive on tabular data [3,10], but also show that on a strongly seasonal and persistent series the margin over naive baselines is smaller than the headline R² values suggest, and that it depends on how the baseline is defined and on the evaluation level, which supports calls for scaled and baseline-relative accuracy measures [8,9].")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6 Limitations
H1("6. Limitations")
BUL(["The target is a modelled estimate; no independent plant-level measurement was used, and the external comparison is at national level with a production index that covers more plants than the 166 studied.",
     "The test period is 13 forecast origins from a single release. Facility-level significance does not imply stability across months, and the month-level tests have low power.",
     "The official index values come from a single release and May 2026 is provisional; later revisions may change the comparison slightly.",
     "Hyper-parameters were fixed rather than tuned, and the three tree-based learners were not separated statistically.",
     "The inventory may be revised in later releases; a real forecaster would use the data vintage available at the time, which this retrospective design does not reproduce.",
     "Zero-emission months and the onset of the monsoon dip are poorly predicted, and the 12-month results rest on shorter training histories.",
     "The audit flags exact repeats but cannot show why they occur; attribution to forward-filling is an inference from the documentation [2]."])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7 Conclusions
H1("7. Conclusions")
P(f"A time-ordered, leakage-safe benchmark of monthly facility-level CO₂ forecasts for 166 Indian cement plants shows that machine-learning models reduce absolute error by about {ml_lo:.0f}–{max(abs(rel(m)) for m in [lr,rf,hg,rc,hc]):.0f}% relative to persistence, with the improvement concentrated in larger plants and rare large deviations rather than being uniform across all strata. Intervals achieve approximately nominal aggregate coverage but remain imperfect for zero-emission months and individual groups. At national level, a seasonal-naive forecast is as good or better. An audit and an external comparison with official cement production show that the series reproduces the seasonal cycle but not year-on-year growth, and that part of it repeats prior-year values. The practical conclusion is that forecasts of this inventory are most useful for anomaly screening and gap filling, and that future work should test them against measured plant data and process variables, add rainfall and demand drivers for the monsoon months, and model shutdowns separately.")

